body:
Makes sense to me.  Same thing happens with:

```vim
:enew
:w filename with spaces
```

This is where it's happening: [ex_docmd.c#L4020-L4039](https://github.com/neovim/neovim/blob/25427ae892ccbb537a718c8abfbd4c79d9f94091/src/nvim/ex_docmd.c#L4020-L4039)

```c
    // Replace any other wildcards, remove backslashes.
#ifdef UNIX
    /*
     * Only for Unix we check for more than one file name.
     * For other systems spaces are considered to be part
     * of the file name.
     * Only check here if there is no wildcard, otherwise
     * ExpandOne() will check for errors. This allows
     * ":e `ls ve*.c`" on Unix.
     */
    if (!has_wildcards)
      for (p = eap->arg; *p; ++p) {
        /* skip escaped characters */
        if (p[1] && (*p == '\\' || *p == Ctrl_V))
          ++p;
        else if (ascii_iswhite(*p)) {
          *errormsgp = (char_u *)_("E172: Only one file name allowed");
          return FAIL;
        }
      }
#endif
```

I'm not exactly sure how advantageous ``:e `ls ve*.c` `` is considering tab completion works pretty well and it'll show the error `E77: Too many files` if the glob matches more than one.  It also displays `E79: Cannot expand wildcards` if the glob matches nothing.
